# LinkedIn automation — Cursor-only workflow

Part of **Sahil's Personal Automations**. All paths below are relative to **`LinkedIn Automation/`** (this folder).

**Cursor writes the content. Sahil pastes it into LinkedIn.** No Python. No Playwright. No API key. No cron.

---

## CRITICAL — Never reference Sahil's employer or current work

Coworkers, managers, and recruiters at Sahil's current company follow this account. Treat every post, comment, and connect note as **public, attributable, and potentially read by his employer**. This rule overrides voice tone, anecdote warmth, and "feels more authentic". Lose the war story before losing your job.

**Forbidden — never write:**

- First-person plural that implies current employment: `we`, `our team`, `our app`, `our grid`, `we shipped`, `we run`, `we deploy`, `we wrapped`, `we had`, `we cut`, `this sprint`, `last sprint`, `last week we`, `on our dashboard`, `our operator`, `our admin UI`, `our fleet`
- Specific product surfaces that could identify the employer: `operator tablet`, `floor tablet`, `fleet dashboard`, `the admin UI`, internal tool names, codenames, project nicknames
- Company names, client names, customer names, deal sizes, headcount, org structure, ticket IDs, internal URLs, Slack channels, repo names, private SDK names, internal library names
- Numbers tied to one workplace: rps, MAU, ARR, row counts, latency SLOs, on-call rotation size — anything a competitor or recruiter could fingerprint
- Anything from `content/blocked-phrases.txt`

**Allowed — phrase it as generic experience, teaching, or hypothetical:**

- Past, employer-agnostic: `I've shipped real-time dashboards…`, `I've debugged this pattern before…`, `A few years back I…`, `Early in my frontend career…`
- Hypothetical / archetypal: `Imagine a dashboard that…`, `Picture an operator view with…`, `A common pattern I've seen is…`, `Take a virtualized grid with 800+ live rows…`
- Side-project / learning: `On a side dashboard I built to learn X…`, `When I was profiling this on my laptop…`
- Public stack names (React, TypeScript, AG Grid, Chart.js, WebSockets, V8) — these are industry vocabulary, not employer signal
- Second-person teaching: `you can`, `you should`, `the senior habit is`, `the fix is`

**Rewrite test (apply before saving any draft):** Could someone at Sahil's current company read this and identify a project, customer, teammate, metric, or product surface they recognize? If yes, **rewrite as past-tense personal or hypothetical** until they can't.

**Self-audit prompts when rewriting:**

1. Replace every `we` with `I` (past) or `you` (teaching) or `imagine` (hypothetical). If the sentence breaks, the sentence was employer-attached.
2. Replace any product noun (`operator tablet`, `fleet view`, `admin grid`) with an archetype (`a low-spec tablet`, `a real-time admin view`, `a virtualized grid`).
3. Remove time markers that imply current employment (`this sprint`, `last week we`, `we just rolled out`). Replace with timeless framing or distant past (`a few years back`).

