## Weekly article — frontend system design (every Wednesday)

**Cadence:** once a week, **every Wednesday**, starting **Wednesday 2026-06-17**. This **replaces** that day's normal short post when Sahil runs **`linkedin automation`**. Also runs on explicit asks: "the weekly article" / "system design article" / "this week's deep dive".

**Goal:** a long-form, Medium-ready breakdown of one **frontend system design problem**. Deep, teaching-voice content like this is the strongest signal to hiring managers and top voices that Sahil thinks at a senior level. Same employer-safety and reach rules apply.

### What to produce each week

1. **The article** → `articles/YYYY-MM-DD.md` — a complete Medium-ready post.
   - **Topic:** one frontend system design problem (e.g. design a real-time dashboard, an autocomplete/typeahead service, an infinite-feed with virtualization, a collaborative editor, an image-heavy gallery, a notification system, a design-system component API, offline-first sync, a file-upload pipeline). Pick something **not covered in the last ~8 weeks** of articles. Search the web first for a fresh, relevant angle.
   - **Structure:** title + 1-line subtitle → the problem & why it's hard → requirements (functional + non-functional) → high-level architecture → component/data-flow breakdown → key tradeoffs and failure modes → performance/scale notes → a short "what I'd ship first" conclusion. ~1000–1800 words.
   - **Format:** this file is for Medium, so **standard Markdown is fine here** (real `#` headings, `**bold**`, `code` fences) — the LinkedIn no-Markdown / Unicode-bold rule does **NOT** apply to the article file. It applies only to the LinkedIn companion post.
   - **Voice & safety:** still Sahil's voice (easy English, no em dash in body, peer-teaching) and still employer-safe (past-personal, hypothetical, teaching — no `we`/`our`/current work, no internal product surfaces).

2. **Images (required)** → save to `articles/assets/YYYY-MM-DD-*.png` and embed them in the article with Markdown image syntax.
   - **At least one architecture / data-flow diagram** is mandatory. Prefer generating a clean diagram (e.g. via the diagram tooling or the image generation tool) — boxes, arrows, layers. A sequence diagram for the critical flow is a strong second image.
   - Add 1–2 supporting visuals where they help (component tree, before/after, state machine). Use real diagrams, not decorative stock art.
   - Embed in the article as `![alt text](assets/YYYY-MM-DD-architecture.png)` so they render on the local file and travel when pasted/uploaded to Medium.

3. **The LinkedIn companion post** → `drafts/YYYY-MM-DD.md` — a short teaser that drives people to the article.
   - Follows **all** normal LinkedIn rules (Unicode bold, no em dash, hook, 150–350 words, one question, 12–15 hashtags per § Hashtags in `content/VOICE.md`).
   - Tease the problem and 2–3 sharp takeaways, then point readers to the full breakdown. **LinkedIn suppresses reach on posts with external links in the body**, so put the article link in the **first comment**, and end the post with a line like "Full breakdown with diagrams in the comments." Note the intended link target in an HTML comment at the top of the draft for Sahil.
   - Upload the lead diagram as the LinkedIn post image too (mention this in the copy-paste checklist).

### After producing

- Print a copy-paste checklist: article path, image paths, LinkedIn draft path, and the reminder to (a) publish the Medium article first, (b) paste the LinkedIn teaser, (c) drop the article link in the first comment, (d) attach the lead diagram.
- When Sahil says "I posted it", log it to `storage/post-history.jsonl` with a `"format": "article"` field so weekly deep-dives are easy to spot and not repeated.

